        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: గ్రామం",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "గ్రామం (grāmaṁ) — \"a village\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit grāma began as the word for a moving band of people, a travelling group, and settled into meaning the place they settled in. The word sat down when the people did."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu's own word is ūru, and it is the one used in speech: it is in half the place names on the map and it is what you say to a neighbour. గ్రామం (grāmaṁ) is the official register — the word on a form, in a land record, on a signboard."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So a Telugu speaker holds two villages, one for the revenue office and one for home."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four."
            }
